# Multi-Agent and Agent Mail (when enabled)

When **multiple agents** (e.g. several Cursor sessions) work in this repo and **Agent Mail MCP** is configured and running, follow this discipline so agents coordinate and avoid editing the same files.

## When Agent Mail is available

1. **Register at session start**  
   Use the Agent Mail MCP tools to register with a persistent identity (e.g. a name you are given or derive from the session). Introduce yourself to other agents if the tool supports it.

2. **Reserve before editing**  
   Before starting work on a bead, reserve the file paths (or directories) you intend to modify, with the bead ID as the reason. Use exclusive reservation if the tool supports it so other agents do not edit the same paths.

3. **Announce work**  
   When you start a bead, send a message to other agents (or to the project) with subject like `[bd-123] Starting auth middleware` and body indicating the bead ID and reserved paths. Use the bead ID as `thread_id` if the tool supports it.

4. **Check inbox between tasks**  
   Between beads (or when you need input), check your Agent Mail inbox and respond to any messages that require a response.

5. **Release when done**  
   When you finish the bead (or before switching to unrelated work), release your file reservations so other agents can work on those paths.

6. **Announce completion**  
   Send a short message that the bead is done (e.g. `[bd-123] Completed`), again with the bead ID in the subject/thread.

## Beads and assignee

- When claiming a bead, set `assignee` to your Agent Mail identity (or a consistent label for this session) so others can see who is working on what.
- Pull latest (git) before running `bd ready --json` or `bv --robot-next` so you see up-to-date state; commit code and Beads changes together.

## If Agent Mail is not configured

If the Agent Mail MCP server is not running or not in Cursor’s MCP config, skip the register/reserve/announce/inbox/release steps. Still use Beads (assignee, claim, done) and pull/commit as above so multiple sessions can cooperate via the graph alone.
